exam: [
 {cmd:"Define", m:2,
  q:"Define 'joint venture'.",
  ctx:"",
  plan:["Two or more businesses start a new project together.","Add: sharing capital, risk and profit."],
  model:["A joint venture is when two or more businesses agree to start a new project together (1), sharing the capital needed, the risks involved and the profits earned (1)."],
  marker:"1 mark for 'two or more businesses working together on a project'; 1 for the sharing of capital, risk or profit."},

 {cmd:"Outline", m:4,
  q:"Outline two possible reasons why KKosmetics wants to enter new markets abroad.",
  ctx:"KKosmetics manufactures make-up for women and sells across Europe. The Marketing Director says: 'We are reaching saturation in the European markets and we need to increase sales in new markets.'",
  plan:["Reason 1: saturation at home — stated directly in the case.","Reason 2: growth potential in African markets as incomes rise.","Use the case wording."],
  model:[
   "The European market is reaching saturation (1), which means sales there have stopped growing and can now only be increased by taking customers from competitors — an expensive way to grow — so KKosmetics must look elsewhere if it wants sales to rise (1).",
   "African markets offer far greater growth potential (1). Incomes in many African countries are rising quickly and the cosmetics market there is much less developed, so the same marketing effort is likely to generate more additional sales than it would in a saturated European market (1)."],
  marker:"4 marks. The first reason is quoted directly from the case — always take the planted evidence first, then add one of your own."},

 {cmd:"Explain", m:6,
  q:"Explain two possible problems for KKosmetics of entering new markets abroad.",
  ctx:"KKosmetics manufactures make-up for women and is planning to sell in African countries.",
  plan:["Problem 1: cultural differences — cosmetics are highly culture- and skin-tone specific; advertising imagery restricted in some countries.","Problem 2: lack of knowledge of distribution — where do consumers actually buy cosmetics in these markets?","Analyse each to a sales consequence."],
  model:[
   "Cultural differences are a serious problem for a cosmetics business specifically. Make-up shades must match the skin tones of the local population, and a range developed for European customers may be almost unusable in parts of Africa. Beyond the product itself, advertising for cosmetics relies heavily on images of women, and in some countries there are cultural or legal restrictions on how women may be depicted. KKosmetics could therefore find that neither its product range nor its existing advertising can be used unchanged, which means real development cost before a single sale is made.",
   "KKosmetics also lacks knowledge of how cosmetics are actually distributed in these markets. In Europe it may sell through pharmacy chains and department stores, but in many African countries consumers buy from small independent traders and open markets rather than large retail chains. Without local knowledge KKosmetics could choose the wrong distribution channel entirely, so that its products are simply not on sale where its customers shop — and the whole marketing investment is wasted."],
  marker:"6 marks: 3 per problem. Both are made specific to **cosmetics** — skin tones, depiction of women, where make-up is bought. Generic 'different culture and language' answers score 4 at most."},

 {cmd:"Justify", m:6,
  q:"Do you think these problems can be easily overcome by KKosmetics? Justify your answer.",
  ctx:"The Managing Director would prefer to expand in Africa by forming a joint venture.",
  plan:["Can be overcome: joint venture supplies exactly the missing local knowledge (distribution, culture, advertising norms); product can be localised with new shades; franchising/licensing alternatives exist.","Cannot easily: 'Africa' is not one market — 54 countries with different cultures, languages, currencies and regulations; joint ventures bring management conflict and shared profit; developing new shade ranges is expensive; exchange rate risk remains.","Judgement: partly — with a qualifier about treating Africa as multiple markets."],
  model:[
   "Several of the problems can be addressed, and the Managing Director's preferred method is well chosen. A joint venture with a local partner supplies precisely what KKosmetics lacks: knowledge of where consumers buy cosmetics, what shades and formulations suit local skin tones, and what advertising is culturally acceptable. The partner also shares the capital and the risk, so a market that does not develop is less costly than if KKosmetics had built its own operation there.",
   "But the problems should not be called *easy* to overcome. Africa is not a single market — it is more than fifty countries with different languages, cultures, currencies, income levels and regulations, so a joint venture that gives KKosmetics excellent knowledge of one country tells it very little about the next. Developing new shade ranges is a genuine manufacturing cost, not a marketing adjustment. And joint ventures bring their own difficulties: management conflict between two businesses with different cultures, and profits that must be shared with the partner.",
   "My judgement is that the problems are manageable but not easily overcome, and the decisive point is the scale of what 'entering African markets' actually means. KKosmetics should not attempt the continent at once; it should choose **one or two countries** with the largest and fastest-growing cosmetics markets, form a joint venture in each, and develop a localised shade range for them before expanding further. Treated that way the problems are solvable. Treated as a single market entry, they are not — and the cost of getting the product range and the distribution wrong across many countries simultaneously could be severe for a business whose European sales are already flat."],
  marker:"6 marks. The insight that lifts this answer is challenging the premise that 'Africa' is one market. Recognising that the question's framing is itself flawed, and proposing a staged entry, is exactly what the top evaluation band rewards."}
]
